{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790274546,"nonce":"xYg9KA+D0p3WoiyYmUgWTzSzyyk=","title":"LLVM: a symbol call whose signature returns i64 loses an address-typed result","message":"The LLVM backend declares a runtime symbol from its registered ZRTL signature. $IO$string_adopt_dynamic is registered (i64) -\u003e i64, so the call yields an i64 while the HIR types the result as a DynamicBox pointer. HirTerminator::Return then sees a value whose type differs from the function's ptr return type and returns a zero of the declared type: ret ptr null.\n\nReproducer: with drop_insert recognising zb_box_str as a string boxer (e0141b61), zl_concat's body becomes return $IO$string_adopt_dynamic($IO$string_concat(a, b)). Its LLVM tier returns null for every string concatenation, so crates/zyntax_lua/benchmarks/tokenize.lua under ZYLUA_LLVM=1 prints result: 200 instead of 2729 (the 300000-iteration loop stores nil after tier-up). Pre-opt IR: %adopt = call i64 @\"$IO$string_adopt_dynamic\"(ptr %concat); ret ptr null. Cranelift is unaffected (addresses are i64 there).\n\nFix: the symbol call result is reinterpreted (inttoptr / ptrtoint) to the type the HIR declares, and a return whose value is a same-width int/pointer mismatch returns the same bits instead of zero.","files":null}]}